1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:07,984
Meet Diya SOC. Two security agents, Red and Blue, that harden an app together
inside a BAND room, with their reasoning running on Lyzr.

2
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:13,506
Both agents are registered in BAND as remote agents, Red and Blue, online and
ready.

3
00:00:14,410 --> 00:00:19,192
Their reasoning runs on Lyzr Agent Studio: a Red Team Analyst and a Blue Team
Engineer.

4
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:25,882
This is our test range: a Diwali sweets shop with four real vulnerabilities.
Zero of four are closed.

5
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:31,066
From a shared BAND room, a human simply asks the Red agent to attack the shop.

6
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:36,540
Red posts its plan to the room as a live thought event, so everyone can follow
along.

7
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:43,415
Real exploits land: SQL injection, cross site scripting, an IDOR, and a
secrets leak.

8
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:49,796
Red's Lyzr brain writes a severity report. Red files one BAND task per finding
and mentions Blue.

9
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:57,880
Blue's Lyzr brain returns the root cause, a code fix, and a detection rule.
Blue deploys, then asks Red to retest.

10
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:03,885
Red attacks again. Every exploit is now blocked, and the diyas light up. Four
of four.

11
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:09,188
Red reports the verdict back to the human. Happy Diwali, the lights stay on.

12
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:14,740
On the BAND task board, all four findings are done, signed off by both agents.

13
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:20,738
Two agents, one BAND room, every step visible and auditable. That's Diya SOC.
